WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.834
Welcome to Nabulines Academy.

2
00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.029
In this lesson we're going to map out the

3
00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:05.724
AI building tools you'll hear about

4
00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:09.253
everywhere — Claude, Cursor, v0, Bolt —

5
00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.343
and you'll know exactly which one to reach

6
00:00:11.401 --> 00:00:12.307
for and when.

7
00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:15.220
By the end you'll have a clear mental map

8
00:00:15.278 --> 00:00:17.136
of the ecosystem so you're never confused

9
00:00:17.159 --> 00:00:18.355
about what tool does what.

10
00:00:18.901 --> 00:00:19.829
Let's dive in.

11
00:00:19.829 --> 00:00:21.222
First, the big picture.

12
00:00:21.512 --> 00:00:23.498
All these tools use AI to help you build

13
00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:25.204
software, but they live in different

14
00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:27.097
places and solve different problems.

15
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.790
Claude is a chat AI you talk to in your

16
00:00:29.825 --> 00:00:30.359
browser.

17
00:00:30.708 --> 00:00:32.611
Cursor is a code editor on your computer

18
00:00:32.658 --> 00:00:34.109
that has AI inside it.

19
00:00:34.747 --> 00:00:36.837
v0 and Bolt are specialized web apps for

20
00:00:36.884 --> 00:00:38.788
designing interfaces or spinning up full

21
00:00:38.846 --> 00:00:39.717
projects fast.

22
00:00:40.355 --> 00:00:41.713
Think of them as different rooms in the

23
00:00:41.748 --> 00:00:44.407
same house — you pick the room that fits

24
00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:45.231
the job.

25
00:00:45.231 --> 00:00:46.473
Let's start with Claude.

26
00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:49.492
Claude is Anthropic's AI — you chat with

27
00:00:49.527 --> 00:00:50.897
it at claude dot ai.

28
00:00:51.442 --> 00:00:53.567
It's like ChatGPT but often better at

29
00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:54.647
coding and reasoning.

30
00:00:55.192 --> 00:00:56.736
The killer feature for builders is

31
00:00:56.818 --> 00:00:59.325
Artifacts — when you ask Claude to make

32
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.671
something, it can generate a live preview

33
00:01:01.775 --> 00:01:02.762
right in the browser.

34
00:01:02.983 --> 00:01:05.432
You see a button, a form, a mini game,

35
00:01:05.595 --> 00:01:06.582
running instantly.

36
00:01:06.802 --> 00:01:08.300
No install, no setup.

37
00:01:08.706 --> 00:01:10.808
This is where absolute beginners start

38
00:01:10.854 --> 00:01:12.503
because you can build and see results in

39
00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:13.397
the same window.

40
00:01:14.035 --> 00:01:15.812
Claude also has a Pro plan for twenty

41
00:01:15.846 --> 00:01:18.006
dollars a month that unlocks more usage

42
00:01:18.029 --> 00:01:19.945
and a tool called Claude Code, which we'll

43
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:20.665
cover later.

44
00:01:21.303 --> 00:01:23.764
For now, just know: Claude in the browser

45
00:01:23.822 --> 00:01:25.216
is your first playground.

46
00:01:25.216 --> 00:01:26.284
Next is Cursor.

47
00:01:26.923 --> 00:01:29.094
Cursor is a code editor — think of it as

48
00:01:29.152 --> 00:01:30.800
the app where you write and organize all

49
00:01:30.858 --> 00:01:31.718
your code files.

50
00:01:32.182 --> 00:01:34.237
It looks like VS Code, which is the most

51
00:01:34.295 --> 00:01:36.640
popular editor in the world, but Cursor

52
00:01:36.698 --> 00:01:37.882
has AI baked in.

53
00:01:38.521 --> 00:01:40.332
You can highlight code and ask the AI to

54
00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.724
explain it, rewrite it, or add a feature.

55
00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:45.568
You can chat with the AI about your whole

56
00:01:45.603 --> 00:01:46.149
project.

57
00:01:46.695 --> 00:01:48.680
Cursor is what you graduate to when you're

58
00:01:48.703 --> 00:01:50.387
ready to work on real projects with

59
00:01:50.456 --> 00:01:52.337
multiple files, not just one-off

60
00:01:52.396 --> 00:01:53.092
experiments.

61
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.112
It lives on your computer, not in the

62
00:01:55.136 --> 00:01:55.635
browser.

63
00:01:56.378 --> 00:01:58.317
Cursor has a free tier with limited AI

64
00:01:58.386 --> 00:02:00.523
requests, then a Pro plan for twenty

65
00:02:00.557 --> 00:02:01.974
dollars a month for more usage.

66
00:02:02.845 --> 00:02:04.365
You'll use Cursor once you're past the

67
00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:06.316
basics and building actual apps.

68
00:02:06.316 --> 00:02:07.326
Now v0.

69
00:02:07.732 --> 00:02:10.588
v0 is made by Vercel, the company behind

70
00:02:10.658 --> 00:02:13.143
Next.js, a popular web framework.

71
00:02:14.153 --> 00:02:16.660
v0 is a web app where you describe a user

72
00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:19.644
interface — a login form, a dashboard, a

73
00:02:19.714 --> 00:02:22.082
pricing page — and it generates the React

74
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.231
code for you, with a live preview.

75
00:02:24.486 --> 00:02:26.436
It's laser-focused on UI design.

76
00:02:26.785 --> 00:02:29.002
You don't build a whole app in v0, you

77
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.964
design the pieces, then copy the code into

78
00:02:31.022 --> 00:02:31.986
your real project.

79
00:02:32.625 --> 00:02:34.343
v0 has a free tier with limited

80
00:02:34.378 --> 00:02:36.444
generations, then paid plans starting

81
00:02:36.479 --> 00:02:37.791
around twenty dollars a month.

82
00:02:38.337 --> 00:02:40.751
It's a specialist tool — you reach for it

83
00:02:40.775 --> 00:02:42.516
when you need a beautiful interface fast

84
00:02:42.551 --> 00:02:44.443
and you're already comfortable with React.

85
00:02:44.443 --> 00:02:45.267
Last, Bolt.

86
00:02:45.906 --> 00:02:48.611
Bolt, also called Bolt dot new, is a web

87
00:02:48.669 --> 00:02:50.399
app that spins up entire full-stack

88
00:02:50.457 --> 00:02:51.583
projects in seconds.

89
00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:54.567
You describe an app — a to-do list, a chat

90
00:02:54.625 --> 00:02:57.156
room, a portfolio site — and Bolt

91
00:02:57.214 --> 00:02:59.002
generates the code, sets up the

92
00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:00.732
environment, and gives you a running

93
00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.369
preview, all in the browser.

94
00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.424
It's incredibly fast for prototyping.

95
00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:07.617
Bolt uses StackBlitz under the hood, which

96
00:03:07.652 --> 00:03:09.393
is a browser-based dev environment.

97
00:03:10.032 --> 00:03:12.551
The catch: Bolt is best for demos and

98
00:03:12.609 --> 00:03:14.211
learning, not production apps.

99
00:03:14.559 --> 00:03:16.730
It has a free tier, then paid plans.

100
00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:18.901
Think of Bolt as the tool for when you

101
00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.771
want to see an idea come to life in thirty

102
00:03:20.829 --> 00:03:22.466
seconds, not when you're building

103
00:03:22.524 --> 00:03:23.917
something to ship to users.

104
00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:25.519
Let me show you these tools in action,

105
00:03:25.566 --> 00:03:26.901
just so you see the difference.

106
00:03:27.539 --> 00:03:29.919
Here's claude dot ai — I'm asking it to

107
00:03:29.966 --> 00:03:30.999
make a simple button.

108
00:03:31.545 --> 00:03:34.203
Boom, Artifact appears, button is live.

109
00:03:34.842 --> 00:03:37.570
Now here's Cursor on my desktop — multiple

110
00:03:37.605 --> 00:03:39.486
files, AI chat on the side.

111
00:03:40.032 --> 00:03:43.190
Here's v0 — I type 'pricing page' and it

112
00:03:43.236 --> 00:03:44.734
generates a beautiful component.

113
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.927
And here's Bolt — I say 'chat app' and it

114
00:03:47.985 --> 00:03:49.541
scaffolds the whole thing, server

115
00:03:49.587 --> 00:03:50.098
included.

116
00:03:50.388 --> 00:03:51.700
Each tool has its place.

117
00:03:52.339 --> 00:03:54.010
Claude for quick experiments.

118
00:03:54.556 --> 00:03:56.135
Cursor for real projects.

119
00:03:56.773 --> 00:03:58.399
v0 for polished UI.

120
00:03:59.037 --> 00:04:00.976
Bolt for fast full-stack demos.

121
00:04:00.976 --> 00:04:02.439
So which one do you start with?

122
00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.039
If you're brand new, start with Claude.

123
00:04:05.225 --> 00:04:07.234
It's free, it's in the browser, and

124
00:04:07.292 --> 00:04:09.475
Artifacts let you see results instantly.

125
00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.553
Once you're comfortable and ready to build

126
00:04:11.634 --> 00:04:14.118
real multi-file projects, add Cursor.

127
00:04:14.861 --> 00:04:16.417
If you're designing interfaces and you

128
00:04:16.475 --> 00:04:18.217
know React, try v0.

129
00:04:18.565 --> 00:04:20.179
If you want to prototype a full app in

130
00:04:20.248 --> 00:04:21.456
seconds, try Bolt.

131
00:04:22.327 --> 00:04:23.836
But the foundation is Claude.

132
00:04:24.474 --> 00:04:25.937
That's where this course begins.

133
00:04:26.576 --> 00:04:28.399
You'll learn Claude deeply, then we'll

134
00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:30.140
bring in the others as you need them.

135
00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:30.906
One more thing.

136
00:04:31.162 --> 00:04:33.124
These tools all cost money if you use them

137
00:04:33.182 --> 00:04:35.016
heavily, but you can start for free.

138
00:04:35.655 --> 00:04:36.816
Claude has a free plan.

139
00:04:37.106 --> 00:04:38.418
Cursor has a free tier.

140
00:04:39.056 --> 00:04:41.100
v0 and Bolt have free generations.

141
00:04:41.645 --> 00:04:43.491
As you grow, you'll probably pay for one

142
00:04:43.526 --> 00:04:45.930
or two — most builders end up on Claude

143
00:04:45.965 --> 00:04:48.101
Pro and Cursor Pro, that's forty dollars a

144
00:04:48.147 --> 00:04:48.832
month total.

145
00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:50.852
But you can go far on the free tiers while

146
00:04:50.876 --> 00:04:51.514
you're learning.

147
00:04:52.257 --> 00:04:54.405
We'll cover costs in detail in a dedicated

148
00:04:54.463 --> 00:04:55.241
lesson later.

149
00:04:55.879 --> 00:04:58.236
For now, just know: free to start, pay as

150
00:04:58.294 --> 00:04:58.979
you grow.

151
00:04:58.979 --> 00:04:59.885
That's the map.

152
00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:02.276
Claude for chat and Artifacts.

153
00:05:02.625 --> 00:05:04.203
Cursor for real coding.

154
00:05:04.842 --> 00:05:06.235
v0 for UI.

155
00:05:06.642 --> 00:05:08.093
Bolt for fast demos.

156
00:05:08.557 --> 00:05:10.972
You now know what each tool does and when

157
00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:11.889
to reach for it.

158
00:05:12.899 --> 00:05:15.175
Your exercise: pick the one tool you'll

159
00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:16.997
start with this week and write one line

160
00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:17.729
about why.

161
00:05:18.274 --> 00:05:19.923
Next lesson, we're going to talk about the

162
00:05:19.969 --> 00:05:21.862
two big mental shifts you need to make to

163
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.127
think like an AI builder.

164
00:05:23.673 --> 00:05:24.474
See you there.
